om_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0e959-915c-7f63-ba56-125e48a64bf4","call_id":"call_clFye581gUjqfQTHG6Wf0zDq","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"b4d0ac\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000013377,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2131,\"output\":\"# CD3 Hybrid Source and Binary Audit\\n\\n## Result\\n\\nThe two existing rate-1.4 cores expose a concrete under-sized capacity check\\ninside the loaded CUDA driver's batched-copy command emitter. This is more\\nspecific evidence than merely observing a crash in `libcuda` or a successful\\nserialized run. No serving run or synthetic CUDA experiment was performed\\nfor this audit. Production code and experiment settings were not changed.\\n\\nThe application submits variable-length segment spans. The driver checks space\\nusing a cached command-size estimate plus 1,152 bytes, but its inline-copy\\nemitter writes the current payload plus its command header. In both cores,\\nthe checked size fits and the actual emitted size does not.\\n\\n| Existing core | Cached estimate | Checked bytes (+1152) | Writable bytes remaining | Actual bytes needed |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Original pipeline, 20260928T144024Z | 3660 | 4812 | 12108 | 21420 |\\n| Second pipeline, 20260928T181012Z | 6276 | 7428 | 14012 | 16164 |\\n\\nActual bytes needed here are 32 bytes of fixed header, one 4-byte inline\\npacket header, and the current payload: 21,384 or 16,128 bytes respectively.\\nBoth payloads fit in a single emitter chunk (maximum 32,764 bytes).\\n\\nThe immediate failure mechanism is therefore an internal command-buffer\\noverrun, not an application request to copy past the end of a 64-KiB segment.\\nThis audit identifies the failing size calculation in the installed binary;\\nit is not a vendor-confirmed bug report or proof of a particular fixed version.\\n\\n## Application Path\\n\\nSource files in this directory are inspection copies of the current container\\ncheckout, not a second deployed implementation. Baseline commit:\\n`aaa16a378cb1194bb5cdc161e05c81e359dbf48e`, plus the existing Nsight patch and\\nthe already-added opt-in serial diagnostic.\\n\\n1. `tiered_serve/compression/cuda/chunk_delta3_hybrid.h:1021` selects and sorts\\n   exception entries by segment and byte offset. Lines 1051-1071 merge only\\n   adjacent entries in the same segment, producing variable-length copies.\\n2. `chunk_delta3_hybrid.h:543` submits the K and V batches on the H2D stream,\\n   then queues their decoders with K/V completion-event dependencies.\\n3. `tiered_serve/compression/cuda/chunk_delta3_ext.cpp:1023` passes these sizes\\n   to `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync`, with one valid stream-order attribute covering\\n   the batch. CUDA 13's eight-argument signature is used.\\n4. `tiered_serve/compression/cuda/chunk_delta3_csr_serial.h:241` instead adds\\n   each side's contiguous CSR exception arena as one copy. CSR and hybrid\\n   share the GPU decode kernel but differ in exception-copy shape and stream\\n   scheduling. CSR success does not exercise the same descriptor sequence.\\n\\nThe native code does not branch on request rate. A different arrival rate can\\nchange active rows, selected segment spans, copy lengths, and command-buffer\\nposition. The demonstrated failure requires the checked size to fit while\\nthe current emitted command does not. Thus rate 1.6 succeeding does not\\ncontradict this mechanism. Its precise successful command sequence was not\\nreconstructed, so this is a mechanistic explanation, not an observed trace\\nof the avoided boundary in that successful run.\\n\\n## Driver Instructions\\n\\nBinary: `/usr/local/cuda-13.2/compat/libcuda.so.1`, package 595.45.04.\\nSHA-256: `e83bb4f47a079d86ba6c2e04449430b0bc1c852904e2cf53ae1995b2e76c91e0`.\\nAddresses below are the second core's virtual addresses. Function/variable\\nnames in the pseudocode are explanatory, not recovered NVIDIA source names.\\n\\nThe batch loop initializes `[sp+328]` at `0xf525535f638c`; subsequent\\niterations branch to `0xf525535f63a0`, after that initialization.\\n\\n```text\\n0xf525535f6fc4  ldr  w1, [sp, #328]       ; cached size estimate\\n0xf525535f6fcc  cbnz w1, ...7a44\\n0xf525535f7a44  add  w2, w1, #0x480       ; estimate + 1152\\n0xf525535f7a58  bl   0xf525535092b0       ; capacity checker\\n0xf525535f7a68  b.eq ...7b80               ; insufficient-space branch\\n```\\n\\nThe capacity checker uses that argument to update the reserved end position.\\nIt does not receive the current payload length. The emitter, in contrast,\\nreceives the current length from the sizes array, constructs an inline packet,\\nand copies that many bytes into the command buffer.\\n\\n```text\\n0xf52553511b60  add  x6, x0, #0x20        ; fixed header = 32 bytes\\n0xf52553511b88  cmp  w21, w25              ; chunk limit = 32764 bytes\\n0xf52553511bb4  str  w3, [x6], #4         ; inline header = 4 bytes\\n0xf52553511bbc  str  wzr, [x6, x0, lsl #2]; zero last payload word: FAULT\\n0xf52553511bc0  mov  x0, x6\\n0xf52553511bc4  bl   ...                   ; payload memcpy\\n```\\n\\nAfter emission, the caller retains a nonzero cached estimate instead of\\nincreasing it to the size just emitted:\\n\\n```text\\n0xf525535f7024  sub  x25, x0, x25         ; bytes actually emitted\\n0xf525535f7028  ldr  w2, [sp, #328]\\n0xf525535f7030  cmp  w2, #0\\n0xf525535f7034  csel w0, w2, w25, ne      ; retain old nonzero estimate\\n0xf525535f7038  str  w0, [sp, #328]\\n```\\n\\nFor this path, the behavior is equivalent to:\\n\\n```c\\nif (cached_estimate != 0) {\\n    if (!has_capacity(cursor, cached_estimate + 1152))\\n        goto obtain_more_space;\\n}\\nnext = emit_copy(cursor, current_source, current_destination, current_size);\\nif (cached_estimate == 0)\\n    cached_estimate = next - cursor;\\ncursor = next;\\n```\\n\\nThis is a partial reconstruction of the observed branch, not the complete\\ndriver algorithm. Other transfer types and allocation branches exist.\\n\\n## Second Core Arithmetic\\n\\nThe capacity-checker's own retained state independently matches the undersized\\ncheck, rather than only the crash register values:\\n\\n- Command allocation: 4,194,304 bytes, ending at `0x200c00000`.\\n- Current subrange begins at allocation offset 4,089,108 (`0x3e6514`).\\n- Remaining subrange capacity: 105,196 bytes.\\n- Current command cursor: `0x200bfc944`, offset 91,184 within the subrange.\\n- Cached estimate: 6,276 bytes; checked size: 7,428 bytes.\\n- Recorded reserved end: 98,612 (`0x18134`) = 91,184 + 7,428.\\n- Required end: 107,348 = 91,184 + 16,164, exceeding capacity by 2,152 bytes.\\n- Payload begins at `0x200bfc968`; last 4-byte store faults at `0x200c00864`.\\n\\nThe original core has the same instruction and the same under-check pattern:\\ncommand cursor `0x200bfd0b4`, payload start `0x200bfd0d8`, last store\\n`0x200c0245c`. The original source descriptor was independently checked against\\nits live segment: 21,384 bytes within a 65,536-byte segment, matching generation,\\n29 record references, and one reader lease.\\n\\nFull batch-loop disassembly and capacity-checker output are saved alongside\\nthis report. Core analysis reads the existing crashed processes; it does not\\nlaunch the serving workload.\\n\\n## Other Source Checks\\n\\n- Segment append reserves a disjoint range and increments a record reference\\n  before copying; publication occurs after that copy. Reader leases prevent\\n  segment recycling while a plan's H2D remains in flight.\\n- Fixed body writes wait for the layer's previous H2D completion in\\n  `chunk_delta3_kv_compressor.py:1955`. GPU staging reuse waits for the ring's\\n  decode-consumed event in `chunk_delta3_hybrid.h:540`.\\n- CPU exception writes are capacity-bounded; overflow records are handled by\\n  the existing raw fallback. The kernel is shared with CSR. No concrete normal-\\n  path overrun in those routines was found in this inspection.\\n- The process-wide `stack_pool.c:32` and `:48` hooks remain independently unsafe:\\n  the free list lacks synchronization, `munmap` accepts untracked addresses\\n  solely by size, and reuse ignores requested protection/flags. These are real\\n  defects but are not needed to explain the demonstrated under-sized check.\\n- The sibling PyTorch source is clean at the documented commit\\n  `70d99e998b4955e0049d13a98d77ae1b14db1f45`. Source cleanliness alone does not\\n  certify its installed native binary; no new build was performed.\\n\\n## Repair Boundary\\n\\nAn application-side workaround belongs in hybrid H2D submission, not in the\\nCD3 encoding arithmetic, request rate, capture request index, or segment budget.\\nKeep segment ownership and the K/V pipeline dependencies intact and avoid the\\nfailing batched-copy emitter path, for example with explicit per-span H2D\\nsubmission. That alternative has not been applied or validated by this audit,\\nand it may change launch overhead. A driver-version replacement likewise needs\\nindependent validation; no fixed version is asserted here. The serial diagnostic\\nis not a proven fix for this command-size accounting defect.\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"82a18d\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000014528,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":423,\"output\":\"set auto-load off\\nset debuginfod enabled off\\nset print thread-events off\\ncore-file /root/projects/tiered-serve/core\\nframe 7\\npython\\nimport gdb,struct,json\\ninf=gdb.selected_inferior()\\ndef u(addr,n): return struct.unpack('<'+'Q'*n,inf.read_memory(addr,n*8))\\nplan=int(gdb.parse_and_eval('$x19'))\\nstorage=int(gdb.parse_and_eval('$x24'))\\npool=u(storage+16,1)[0]\\nstart,end,_=u(pool+24,3)\\nref_start,ref_end,_=u(storage+256,3)\\nrefs=[u(a,2) for a in range(ref_start,ref_end,16)]\\nsegments=[]\\nfor index,generation in refs:\\n    data,used,references,readers,actual_generation,allocated=u(start+index*48,6)\\n    segments.append((index,data,used,generation,references,readers))\\nbody_spans=u(plan+104,2)\\nentry_spans=u(plan+120,2)\\nentry_words=u(plan+88,2)\\nfor side in (0,1):\\n    batch=json.load(open('/tmp/cd3-crash-batch-%d.json'%side))\\n    invalid=[]\\n    spans=[]\\n    for i in range(body_spans[side],body_spans[side]+entry_spans[side]):\\n        s,n=batch['sources'][i],batch['sizes'][i]\\n        matches=[x for x in segments if x[1]<=s and s+n<=x[1]+x[2]]\\n        if not matches: invalid.append(i)\\n        if side==0 and i==124: print('FAILED_DESCRIPTOR_SEGMENT',matches,'source_offset',s-matches[0][1] if matches else None,'size',n)\\n        spans.append((batch['destinations'][i],n))\\n    base=min(d for d,n in spans)\\n    total=sum(n for d,n in spans)\\n    offsets=u(batch['sources'][body_spans[side]+entry_spans[side]],1723)\\n    print('SIDE',side,'entry_descriptors',len(spans),'out_of_segment_ranges',invalid,'entry_total',total,'expected',entry_words[side]*8,'destination_extent',max(d+n for d,n in spans)-base,'offset_min_max',(min(offsets),max(offsets)))\\nprint('POOL_SEGMENT_BYTES',u(pool+72,1)[0])\\nend\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0e959-6ca4-7b30-bae0-13aa57103f1f","create_time":1790621356.3809977}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
